From c9741bff7c7c9cfe1ba679782c0cc2d17d975165 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 14:22:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Fix red team silent-truncation bug; drop reader-facing
 badges/legend

redteam.py previously made one batched Claude call across every topic's
articles in a single prompt. On a busy day (286 articles today across
17 topics) the required JSON output exceeded max_tokens=8192, so the
response was truncated, JSON parsing failed, and the whole pass was
silently discarded -- every topic fell back to raw, unvetted retrieval
at once, which is exactly the noise the Military Conflict section was
showing (NFL "bombardment" puns, a "Front Line" company press release,
an unrelated home-invasion crime story).

Fixed by making one Claude call per topic instead of one for
everything: each call's output is now bounded to at most 25 articles'
worth of JSON (verified against today's real 286-article dataset with
a stubbed client), a failure on one topic's call only falls that one
topic back to raw retrieval instead of taking every topic down with
it, and failures are now persisted to data/<date>/redteam/_errors.json
(topic, article count, error, stop_reason) instead of only living in a
CI log that expires.

Also, per feedback that the page's badges/legend (wire service, COCOM,
source origin, audience-reach) mattered more to how the pipeline
classifies a story than to a reader trying to read it: build_brief.py
no longer renders any of them. The underlying data (cocom tag,
SOURCE_ORIGIN, TRUSTED_SOURCES ranking) is still computed and still
drives filtering/reassignment/ranking -- it's just not printed on every
article anymore. The "reassigned from <topic>" note stays, since it
explains why an article appears under a given topic.

README updated: Cost section reflects the per-topic call architecture
and its (small) overhead delta, and the source-origin/audience-reach
section now says explicitly that this is backing data, not a page
feature.

